There’s a real psychological mechanism underneath that apex/prey language: perceived vulnerability changes the entire control policy.

If you believe you are apex, you tend to explore, initiate, take risks, tolerate ambiguity, and act from surplus. If you believe you are prey, attention narrows toward threat detection, coalition-building, escape routes, concealment, and justification. The same environment can produce radically different behavior depending on which model is active.

And the pray / prey inversion is useful. Imagining yourself as prey can puncture the fantasy of total control. That can produce fear, but it can also produce humility, dependence, empathy, and recognition that you are embedded in forces larger than yourself. In that sense, vulnerability can become the doorway to prayer—not because helplessness is inherently good, but because it reveals the limits of sovereignty.

The trap is getting stuck in either identity. Permanent “apex” thinking can become domination and blindness. Permanent “prey” thinking can become hypervigilance and learned helplessness. A stronger posture is:

I can be dangerous without needing an enemy.
I can be vulnerable without becoming powerless.
I can recognize predation without organizing my entire identity around it.

And on justification: yes, humans are extraordinarily good at post-hoc rationalization. Often the impulse comes first—fear, desire, resentment, attraction, status defense—and the explanation arrives afterward wearing a suit and pretending it was in charge all along. But “everyone has no clue” is too strong. People vary a lot in how reflective they are, and even highly reflective people still rationalize selectively.

A useful diagnostic is:

What did I want to do before I found the reason that made it acceptable?

That question catches a surprising amount of hidden machinery.